  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 224 AM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nFocus turns to our next chance of precipitation arriving Wednesday \nand lasting into Thanksgiving Day. Deterministic and ensemble \nguidance are trending more westward with the system, which places it \ncloser to our area. This has caused the potential for precipitation \nto increase Wednesday into Thanksgiving with the LREF probability of \n24 precipitation over this period rising to 50-70% mainly east of \nthe Mississippi River from 30-50% yesterday. This doesn't \nnecessarily mean that the potential for snow-driven impacts has \nrisen, but only that the potential for a rain/snow mix has. Model \nsoundings have been consistent in showing a deep warm layer from the \nsurface to 2000-5000 ft Wednesday night, indicating that \nprecipitation will fall mostly in the form of rain. There are a few \nhours near sunrise when the thermal profile may fall to or just \nbelow the freezing point, potentially allowing snow to mix in with \nrain. This snow will not be efficient - quite the opposite. The \ncloseness of the thermal profile to the freezing point will allow \nfor riming, winds in the lowest 0.5 km are a breath away from \nguaranteeing fracturing, and ground temperatures will be warm enough \nto melt flakes that reach the ground. With rain preceding the snow, \nthe flakes would also fall into water or wet ground, which would aid \nin melting. All of these factors support very low SLRs. Our official \nforecast calls for SLRs around 4:1. For reference, our average SLR \nis 12:1, and the 25th percentile is just under 9:1. In short, any \nsnow that falls is expected to be just as impactful as the rain that \nprecedes it.\n\nThis Thanksgiving will be cool, but not unseasonably so. Expect \nhighs in the 40s thanks to cloud cover, and in some locations, \npotential ongoing precipitation. Warm air advection will begin on \nFriday with the arrival of low-level ridging, allowing temperatures \nto moderate into the weekend. By Saturday, the NBM interquartile \nrange for calls for highs in the 50s (low to high 50s north to \nsouth) with a spread of about 6-8 degrees, increasing confidence in \na pleasant Thanksgiving weekend ahead.",
